This session exposed a recurring failure mode in small ARC streams: seeing an isolated marker and a nearby multicolor motif led to repeated unvalidated assumptions about copying, anchor location, orientation, and color substitutions. The correct response is to treat these as separate hypotheses and test them, rather than pivoting directly from one plausible visual story to a submission.

## Distinguish the rule components

For each demo, record:
- the template's exact colored cell coordinates (including its bounding box and holes);
- every candidate marker coordinate and color;
- whether the template is preserved, transformed, or removed in output;
- marker-to-template anchor offset, including whether markers replace a template cell;
- orientation/rotation/reflection, clipping at edges, and overlap behavior;
- whether each target gets the same literal colors or a mapped palette.

Do not use the whole grid's foreground set as the template: unrelated noise marks or additional examples' distractors can contaminate it. Identify the compact structured object by connected components/shape, and separately identify repeated marker class. Compare output against input cell-by-cell to establish which object is source and which coordinates change.

## Validation gate

After each new demo, encode all currently available input/output grids as fixtures and run the exact task file transform on all of them. Require exact dimension and cell equality before spending a submission. If manually transcribing large pairs is error-prone, use compact coordinate fixtures for changed cells only where the background and full grid dimensions are explicitly generated; still verify the reconstructed expected grid exactly.

A syntactically valid transform, a plausible output, or successful execution on only the test input is not evidence. If one candidate fails any demo, do not submit it. Use the next demonstration to discriminate the unresolved anchor/geometry/color possibilities. A failed verdict is expensive and yields no localization, so avoid speculative submissions, especially after several failures.

## Tool/file discipline

Maintain the required task-id program in the actual workspace path supplied by the benchmark. Before modifying a program, read the current file and confirm the working directory/path; accidental writes to a similarly named parent path do not update the active library. Keep experimental hypotheses out of the canonical task file until ready to validate. After verdict, retain only the implementation that reproduces every received demo; otherwise preserve the previous file as the protocol requires.
